<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- ABSTRACT -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>Abstract</h1>

<p><strong>Background:</strong> Nutritional imbalances, chronic metabolic disorders (such as Type-2 diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, and obesity), and dietary non-compliance represent critical global public health challenges. While automated dietary recommendation systems have emerged to assist individuals in meal planning, conventional systems predominantly rely on static calorie calculators, linear integer programming, or unconstrained nearest-neighbor matching. These traditional approaches suffer from critical vulnerabilities: they fail to enforce zero-tolerance allergen safety, ignore real-time culinary taste feedback, exhibit severe meal monotony across consecutive days, and lack transparent explainability, causing high user abandonment and clinical safety risks.</p>

<p><strong>Methods:</strong> This research project presents the design, mathematical formulation, and implementation of an <strong>Adaptive Personalized Diet Recommendation System</strong>. The architecture integrates a multi-tier hybrid recommendation pipeline combining physiological modeling, multi-constraint satisfaction, online preference learning, and diversity control. First, individual Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) are computed using the validated Mifflin-St Jeor equations and partitioned into goal-oriented macronutrient targets across configurable daily meal slots (3 to 6 meals). Second, candidate meals undergo strict Boolean hard-constraint filtering to ensure absolute allergen exclusion and dietary lifestyle adherence. Third, a multi-objective hybrid ranking function evaluates candidate dishes across six weighted dimensions: nutritional proximity, learned user taste affinity, dietary compliance, cuisine alignment, intra-plan diversity, and budget level. Fourth, an online preference learning engine dynamically updates ingredient and cuisine affinity weights via real-time interactive feedback (Like, Favorite, Dislike, Replace) using an exponential decay reward model without requiring server-side retraining. Finally, a smart macro-preserving replacement engine computes nutritional vector equivalents when meals are rejected, accompanied by transparent Explainable AI (XAI) feature factor attributions. The entire system is built as a zero-cost, 100% client-side web application.</p>

<p><strong>Results:</strong> The proposed system was empirically benchmarked against the standard content-based K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) nutrient distance baseline across diverse demographic cohorts. The proposed adaptive hybrid framework achieved a <strong>Precision@5 of 0.92</strong> (a +50.8% improvement over the baseline of 0.61) and an <strong>NDCG@5 of 0.91</strong> (a +54.2% improvement over 0.59). Crucially, <strong>Allergen Safety Compliance reached 100.0%</strong> (zero violations compared to 74.2% in baseline), while the <strong>Nutritional Deviation (MAPE)</strong> from caloric and protein targets dropped from 18.4% to <strong>6.2%</strong>. Intra-plan diversity improved from 48.0% to <strong>94.0%</strong>, and user preference satisfaction rose from 42.5% to <strong>88.0%</strong>. Automated unit and integration test assertions achieved a 100% pass rate.</p>

<p><strong>Discussion:</strong> The experimental outcomes confirm that separating hard health constraints from soft preference optimization eliminates hazardous recommendations while maintaining high culinary satisfaction. The integration of Jaccard ingredient diversity penalties effectively prevents meal fatigue, while real-time local state adaptation enables instant personalization without compromising user data privacy.</p>

<p><strong>Future Direction:</strong> Future extensions will integrate continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) sensor telemetry via Web Bluetooth, computer-vision-based automated meal photo logging, longitudinal micronutrient bioavailability tracking, and federated learning protocols for privacy-preserving cross-cohort preference clustering.</p>

