<h1>Chapter 2 – Literature Survey</h1>

<h2>2.1 Study of Various Approaches in Recommender Systems</h2>
<p>Recommender systems have evolved through Collaborative Filtering (CF), Content-Based Filtering (CBF), Knowledge/Constraint-Based Systems, and Multi-Criteria Hybrid Architectures.</p>

<h2>2.2 Existing Approaches and Frameworks</h2>
<p>Existing literature explores linear programming (MILP), genetic algorithms, and nearest-neighbor vector distance (KNN).</p>

<h2>2.3 Research Gap</h2>
<p>Five critical gaps were identified: (1) Safety Blindness in nearest-neighbor models, (2) Inflexibility to real-time taste feedback, (3) Meal fatigue from lack of variety control, (4) Opacity in black-box models, and (5) Privacy exposure in cloud architectures.</p>

<h2>2.4 Summary of Related Work</h2>
<p class="table-caption">Table 2.1: Comparative Summary of Existing Recommender Approaches and Research Gaps</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Methodology</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Identified Gaps</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Linear Programming</td><td>Mixed-Integer Optimization</td><td>Exact numerical bounds</td><td>Unpalatable food combos; zero preference learning</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Collaborative Filtering</td><td>Matrix Factorization (SVD)</td><td>Community trends</td><td>Extreme cold-start; zero safety guarantee</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Content-Based KNN</td><td>Euclidean Nutrient Vector</td><td>Fast runtime</td><td>25.8% allergen failures; extreme repetition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Genetic Algorithms</td><td>Multi-Objective Evolution</td><td>Explores combos</td><td>Slow convergence; non-deterministic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Knowledge Ontologies</td><td>OWL Rules & Logic</td><td>Reliable constraints</td><td>Static; no taste adaptation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Commercial Apps</td><td>Manual Calorie Logging</td><td>Large food databases</td><td>Passive tracking only; cloud privacy exposure</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Proposed System</strong></td><td><strong>Multi-Objective Hybrid + Hard Filtering + Online Learning</strong></td><td><strong>100% safety; 6.2% MAPE; real-time learning; zero cost</strong></td><td><strong>Addresses all five research gaps</strong></td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- CHAPTER 3 -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>Chapter 3 – Methodology</h1>

<h2>3.1 Design of the Project</h2>

<h3>3.1.1 Data / Input Description</h3>
<p>The system integrates user anthropometric profiles and a curated catalog based on USDA FoodData Central and ICMR-NIN Indian Food Composition Tables (IFCT 2017).</p>

<h3>3.1.2 Model / Framework Description</h3>
<p>Built as a single-page reactive application using modular ES6+ architecture and localStorage persistence.</p>

<h3>3.1.3 Algorithms and Mathematical Models</h3>

<p><strong>1. Anthropometric Energy Formulation:</strong></p>
<p>BMR is computed using the Mifflin-St Jeor formula:</p>
<p style="text-align:center;"><em>E</em><sub>BMR</sub> = 10 · <em>W</em> + 6.25 · <em>H</em> - 5 · <em>A</em> + <em>s</em></p>
<p>where <em>s</em> = +5 for males and -161 for females. Total Daily Energy Expenditure:</p>
<p style="text-align:center;"><em>E</em><sub>TDEE</sub> = <em>E</em><sub>BMR</sub> · &alpha;</p>

<p class="table-caption">Table 3.1: Physical Activity Multipliers (&alpha;)</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Activity Level</th><th>Multiplier &alpha;</th><th>Clinical Definition</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Sedentary</td><td>1.200</td><td>Little or no exercise; desk job</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Lightly Active</td><td>1.375</td><td>Light exercise 1–3 days/week</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Moderately Active</td><td>1.550</td><td>Moderate exercise 3–5 days/week</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Very Active</td><td>1.725</td><td>Heavy exercise 6–7 days/week</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Extra Active</td><td>1.900</td><td>Heavy physical labor or conditioning</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Daily target calorie calculation:</p>
<p style="text-align:center;"><em>E</em><sub>target</sub> = max(1300, <em>E</em><sub>TDEE</sub> + &Delta;<sub>goal</sub>)</p>

<p><strong>2. Hard-Constraint Safety Filter:</strong></p>
<p style="text-align:center;">&Phi;<sub>allergen</sub>(<em>f</em>) = (<em>A</em><sub>user</sub> ∩ <em>A</em><sub>food</sub> = &empty;)</p>

<p><strong>3. Multi-Objective Hybrid Ranking Formula:</strong></p>
<p style="text-align:center;"><em>S</em><sub>final</sub>(<em>f</em>) = 0.35 · <em>S</em><sub>nut</sub> + 0.25 · <em>S</em><sub>pref</sub> + 0.15 · <em>S</em><sub>diet</sub> + 0.10 · <em>S</em><sub>cuis</sub> + 0.10 · <em>S</em><sub>div</sub> + 0.05 · <em>S</em><sub>budg</sub></p>

<p><strong>4. Online Preference Learning:</strong></p>
<p style="text-align:center;">&omega;<sub>i</sub> &larr; max(-1.0, min(1.0, &omega;<sub>i</sub> + &Delta;<em>r</em>))</p>
<p>where &Delta;<em>r</em> = +0.25 for Like, +0.45 for Favorite, -0.40 for Dislike, and -0.25 for Replace.</p>

<p><strong>5. Smart Macro-Preserving Meal Replacement:</strong></p>
<p>Minimizes the 4D normalized Euclidean distance vector between candidate and rejected meals.</p>

<h3>3.1.4 Evaluation Metrics</h3>
<p>Evaluated using Precision@K, Recall@K, NDCG@K, Allergen Safety Compliance (%), Nutritional Deviation MAPE (%), Intra-Plan Diversity (%), and Preference Satisfaction Rate (%).</p>

